189. Rotate Array

class Solution:
    def rotate(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> None:
        n=len(nums)
        k=k%n
        def reverse(left,right):
            while left<=right:
                nums[left],nums[right]=nums[right],nums[left]
                left+=1
                right-=1

        reverse(0,n-1)
        reverse(0,k-1)
        reverse(k,n-1)
        
Logic to Remember
Rotate by k means the last k elements move to the front.

First, reduce unnecessary rotations:

k = k % n
Then perform 3 reverses:
Reverse the entire array.
Reverse the first k elements.
Reverse the remaining n-k elements.
Example
nums = [1,2,3,4,5,6,7]
k = 3

Reverse all:

[7,6,5,4,3,2,1]

Reverse first k:

[5,6,7,4,3,2,1]

Reverse remaining:

[5,6,7,1,2,3,4]

✅ Answer

🧠 Memory Trick

"Reverse All → Reverse First K → Reverse Rest"

or simply

ARR = All → Right(K) → Rest

Template
k %= n

reverse(0, n-1)
reverse(0, k-1)
reverse(k, n-1)
Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1) (in-place)
